CLAIM - what is claimed, and the rules it is measured by

The claim: the smallest complete Tetris, as a 16-bit x86 DOS .COM program. The current holder is pellsson's tinytris
(https://github.com/pellsson/tinytris, tinytris.com, sha256 0cbd70be...4bfa). Its README calls it the first complete
Tetris in 256 bytes or less and says smaller ones exist but change the game, usually by dropping or changing pieces.
This project draws the same line.

There is no official body for this, so the claim is only as good as its rules and how easy they are to re-check.
Everything below is checked by `make test`, not by eye, and tinytris runs through the same harness.

1. what is measured

- One file, <name>.COM. Its size in bytes is the number. No overlays, no second file; a self-extracting COM is
  measured as the COM.
- Nothing is read from outside the file, or from DOS/BIOS data the program did not put there: no font, no save file,
  no peeking into another program. BIOS and DOS services are allowed.

2. the machine, and what the program relies on

- Real-mode x86 run as a DOS .COM: CS=DS=ES=SS, IP=0x100, SP=0xfffe, colour text mode 80x25 already on (video memory
  at B800:0000), BIOS keyboard services (INT 16h), the timer counter at port 0x40.
- The registers DOS gives a .COM at entry are not relied on: `--profile zeroed` runs with every general register 0
  (make matrix). The program may be loaded at any segment (--seg, make matrix).
- The first 50 bytes of the PSP are the board. microtris overwrites the PSP's INT 20h, memory size and termination
  vectors, so it never returns to DOS: game over is a deliberate freeze with the final board on screen, and the
  machine is reset. (tinytris's board also runs into the PSP.) Check 6 accepts a freeze, an exit or a restart.
- The instruction set level is checked by test/cpu_level.py: capstone over the whole code, plus a run that watches
  every basic block and every store into the program's own code. Both programs use 80186 instructions and nothing
  above. Anything above would be reported as a separate, labelled number.
- Both programs use `aam 7` for the random piece. On Intel and AMD the immediate is the base; the NEC V20/V30 ignore it
  and divide by 10.

3. what "complete" means: six features, each tested (test/features.py)

1  all seven tetrominoes appear, and each spawn is exactly one tetromino
2  gravity: with no input a piece falls one row at a time until it lands
3  move left and right, blocked by the walls and by settled blocks
4  rotation, blocked when it would overlap: four presses return the shape, and at both walls and beside a stack no
   cell is lost or moved into a block
5  line clearing: scripted 1-, 2-, 3- and 4-row clears, then a bot plays hundreds of pieces and every landing is
   compared with a reference model
6  game over: a stack that reaches the spawn point ends play (stop, freeze or restart) with no fault and no garbage

Landing is part of 2 and 5: no landing, no stack. The checks read the screen, not the program's memory: an adapter per
program (test/adapters.py) says where the well is and what a block looks like, and test/tetris_model.py decides what
the board must be.

Why six and not the seven of the Tetris4K archive. The archive lists tinytris with a seventh feature, "pause: pause
emulation", which is the emulator being paused, not the program. tinytris has no key that stops its gravity. A program
with a real pause would be stronger and would be reported separately. The archive also lists tinytris at 251 bytes (an
older build); the repo ships 247, the number measured here.

4. what is not required (same as tinytris)

Scoring, next-piece preview, levels, colours per piece, sound, a title screen, keys other than left, right and rotate,
speed independent of the CPU. Gravity is a free-running counter in both programs, so the fall speed is whatever the CPU
does; `make speed` gives instructions per row of fall.

5. extra evidence (not part of the six; run by `make test`)

test/reach.py      every (shape, column, row) a piece can reach in an empty well: all four cells exist and are inside
                   the well, for all 7 pieces
test/fuzz.py       random keys, idle stretches and keys the game does not use, on random stacks; after every frame the
                   screen is the settled stack plus one whole tetromino, a sideways key moves one column or is really
                   blocked, rotation always turns the same way
test/footprint.py  every store the CPU makes lies in the program's own segment or the first text page; at most one byte
                   of its own code is written (the patched opcode)
test/cpu_level.py  nothing above 80186; every basic block starts on an instruction the static listing found
make matrix        checks 1-6, fuzz and footprint again with zeroed registers, other load segments and seeds
make dosbox        the program under real DOSBox, a second implementation of DOS (not deterministic, so a cross-check)

tinytris passes the six checks. It fails two of the extra ones: it loses a vertical I at the right wall (fuzz), and it
writes about 64 KB over the B800 segment every frame (footprint). `make holder-bug` shows both. microtris passes
both. The six-feature claim is made on the same footing for both programs; the two defects are findings, not part of
the claim.

6. limits

- Speed is CPU-bound. In DOSBox use a fixed cycle count; make play uses 400.
- Game over freezes. The look is a red well, black walls and blocks, text-mode cells, no title, no score.
- The evidence is simulation plus one emulator, not hardware. The checks cannot prove the absence of every bug; they
  show which behaviours were exercised, and how.
